  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nStorm chances and coverage continues to ebb as upper level ridge \ndrifts west reorienting the upper level flow and cutting off the \nmid level moisture flow over Nevada, and shoving it eastward over \nthe four corners region. Saturday and Sunday will see the final \ntwo rounds of shower and thunderstorm chances primarily for far \neastern parts of Elko and White Pine counties. Saturday afternoon \nwill see a 25% chance for thunderstorms with gusty outflow winds \nup to 50 MPH, brief heavy rainfall, lightning, and localized flash\nflooding possible. Sunday afternoon will be the last day where \nstorm chances are greater than 10%, as well as being the most \nconditional for storm development as both moisture and dynamics \nwill be very meager. Monday through next Saturday the ridge \ncontinues is slow drift west eventually setting up over the \nsouthern Great Basin and slow strengthening as it does so. This \nwill introduce a drying trend, as well as support the ongoing \nwarming trend as temperatures gradually warm through the weekend \nreaching a peak next week. Winds will be out of the SW with breezy\nafternoon winds ranging at 10 to 20 MPH with gusts up to 30 MPH \npossible. Temperatures will range in the mid 90s to mid 100s, with\nheat advisories in effect for sections of Elko County for \nSaturday and Sunday, and may need to be expanded as heat impacts \nbuild through the week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for thunderstorm chances ending by \nSunday afternoon.\n\nThere High confidence in the potential for warming temperatures \nthrough the weekend. With a Heat Advisories in effect.\n\nThere is moderate confidence in the evolution of the upper air \npattern after Wednesday as models continue to show high pressure \nremaining over the region.",
